Sun Wah Linux Launches "Project Liberate?
   May 23rd, 01:27 UTC

To Promote True Open Source Software Development and Community Building in 
Asia

May 1, 2005 -- In efforts to encourage and promote the spirit of open 
source collaboration in Asia, Sun Wah Linux today announced the launch of 
Project Liberate - a venture that transforms previously closed source 
software into open source projects and makes them readily available to 
community for their use and study. By offering a variety of easy-to-use 
tools to assist collaborative open source development, Project Liberate 
aims to become the focal point of open source development communities in 
Asia now and beyond.

Initially, Sun Wah Linux will release Maxenger, a Web-based email 
solution; Firebeast, an easy-to-use firewall application; and GroupwareX, 
a groupware calendaring and project management product. These projects 
will be hosted on Sun Wah Linux' new community website, 
https://liberate.sw-linux.com.

"Sun Wah Linux has been an active force in the promotion of an open source 
software (OSS) industry for a while now. With Project Liberate, we are 
able to unite a diverse group of programmers, developers and I.T. gurus 
inside one large community to work together towards the usage, development 
and promotion open standard software," said Alex Banh, Deputy CEO of Sun 
Wah Linux. ??We understand that there are concerns over openness, 
flexibility and the adaptation of Asia open source development. However we 
are confident that Project Liberate is the first step within the OSS 
liberation movement. We believe that Project Liberate will help Asian Free 
and Open Source Software (FOSS) communities become a recognized 
contributor in the global FOSS ecosystem and this will lead to an 
increased number of users coming to share their ideas, visions and 
knowledge in an open manner.??

As the largest Linux solutions provider in China, Sun Wah Linux has had a 
long history of helping to bridge the gap between Chinese and Asian open 
source communities. Project Liberate is yet another example of how Sun Wah 
Linux is leading the industry towards new and exciting boundaries by 
providing innovative and quality products to the public. Over the past 
year, Sun Wah Linux became the first Linux distribution in Asia to comply 
with the global Linux Standard Base (LSB) 2.0. Already this year, Sun Wah 
Linux has co-sponsored China OSS Week 2005, which included the first Asia 
Debian Mini-Conf 2005 and Asia CodeFest 2005. This was tremendously 
successful in connecting open source leaders and developers from all 
around the world to foster collaboration and exchange knowledge with the 
local communities.

In the near future, Sun Wah Linux will actively continue a series of 
initiatives to lead the promotion of OSS. Through tight knit collaboration 
with the wider community, Sun Wah Linux is confident that Project Liberate 
will grow and mature in a healthy manner. New supporting projects will be 
introduced to build on the Project Liberate momentum and encourage more 
community developers to exchange ideas relating to OSS.

"Sun Wah Linux continues to support OSS through the launch of education 
projects because we believe that open source development is not only 
limited to software development, but also widely implemented in education. 
In addition to education, Sun Wah Linux continues to support OSS through 
active participation within the CJK OSS Promotion Forum.?? Concluded 
Banh,??We are fully dedicated to leading the industry in a direction that 
will benefit the marketplace, as well as all those who are actively 
involved in the OSS community in Asia.??
